When it commits | During poll(), every auto.commit.interval.ms | When you call it; blocks until the broker answers | When you call it; returns at once | After the listener has handled every record of a poll | When you call ack.acknowledge()
On a commit error | Logged | Retries retriable errors, then throws | No retry; reported to the callback | Handled by the container | Handled by the container
Main risk | Loss if records are handed to other threads; duplicates after a crash | Latency per commit | An older commit can land after a newer one if you retry it | The whole batch replays after a crash | Forgetting to acknowledge
Use for | Simple tools, never with async processing | Before close or on rebalance | Steady-state commits in a plain consumer loop | Most Spring listeners | Commit exactly after a side effect
// spring.kafka.listener.ack-mode=manual_immediate
@KafkaListener(topics = "orders", groupId = "billing")
public void on(ConsumerRecord<String, Order> rec, Acknowledgment ack) {
    billing.handle(rec.value());
    ack.acknowledge();
}
Rule of thumb: every option is at-least-once if you commit after processing, so make the handler idempotent. Spring Kafka already turns auto-commit off and uses BATCH.

